---@class customerio.CancelBroadcastTriggerResponseTrigger
---@field audience_count? number The number of people in the trigger's audience. For a send without a `rate_limit`, we set it when the send finishes. For a rate-limited send, we set it when the first people enter. It is `0` until then.
---@field broadcast_id? number The ID of the broadcast you cancelled the trigger for. Canceling a trigger doesn't cancel the broadcast itself, so you can trigger it again.
---@field cancelled_at? number Unix timestamp when the trigger was cancelled, or `0` if it hasn't been cancelled.
---@field created_at? number Unix timestamp when you created this trigger.
---@field expires_after? number The send window in seconds that you set when you triggered the broadcast, or `0` if the send has no expiry.
---@field id? number The ID of the trigger you canceled.
---@field processed_at? number Unix timestamp when Customer.io processed the trigger, or `0` if it hasn't. A canceled trigger usually shows `0`. In rare cases the send job starts before it sees the cancel request, exits without sending anything, and stamps this value anyway.
---@field rate_limit? customerio.CancelBroadcastTriggerResponseTriggerRateLimit The rate limit you set when you triggered the broadcast. We omit this field if you didn't set one.
---@field scheduled_at? number Unix timestamp when the trigger was scheduled to send.

---The rate limit you set when you triggered the broadcast. We omit this field if you didn't set one.
