---@class customerio.ScheduleNewsletterRequest
---@field newsletter_id number The identifier of a one-time send.
---@field scheduled_at number Unix timestamp for the send. Use `0` to deschedule. When greater than `0`, the time must be in the future and you must send `timezone` so the UI displays the time correctly.
---@field expires_after? number A send window in seconds measured from the scheduled time, or `0` for no expiry. Customer.io stops delivering the one-time send once the window closes. You can't combine it with `tz_match_enabled`, with send time optimization, or with descheduling (`scheduled_at` of `0`), and on an A/B test it must outlast the test duration.
---@field rate_limit_email_rate? number Maximum number of messages per time period. **NOTE:** Though this states `email_rate`, you can use this for other message channels. Only fixed rate limits are supported, not [daily ramp limits](/messaging/send/broadcasts/one-time-sends/#daily-ramp).
---@field rate_limit_spread? boolean When `true`, spreads messages evenly across the time period. Otherwise, it sends as fast as possible up to the limit in each period.
---@field rate_limit_time_period? customerio.ScheduleNewsletterRequestRateLimitTimePeriod|number Time period in seconds for rate limiting. Must be one of 60 (minute), 3600 (hour), or 86400 (day).
---@field timezone? string [IANA timezone name](/journeys/send/timezones/example-timezones/#region-format) (for example, `America/New_York`). Required when `scheduled_at` is greater than `0`. Not required when descheduling (`scheduled_at` is `0`).
---@field tz_match_enabled? boolean When `true`, sends at the same local time in each recipient's timezone. If it's set to `true` and `rate_limit_time_period` is provided, then the period can't be greater than `3600` (1 hour).

---Time period in seconds for rate limiting. Must be one of 60 (minute), 3600 (hour), or 86400 (day).
---@alias customerio.ScheduleNewsletterRequestRateLimitTimePeriod 60|3600|86400

---@class customerio.ScheduleNewsletterResponse
---@field newsletter? customerio.ScheduleNewsletterResponseNewsletter

---@class customerio.ScheduleNewsletterResponseNewsletter
---@field content_ids? customerio.ScheduleNewsletterResponseNewsletterContentIdsList A list of message variants in a one-time send, where a variant is a translation or A/B test.
---@field created? number The date time when the referenced ID was created.
---@field deduplicate_id? string An identifier in the format `id:timestamp` where the id is for the object you're working with (Automations, Deliveries, Exports, Identities, One-time sends, Segments, and Templates), and the timestamp is the last time the object was updated.
---@field expires_after? number The send window in seconds, if you set one. Customer.io stops delivering the one-time send this long after it starts.
---@field id? number The identifier for a one-time send.
---@field name? string The name of the one-time send. Must be 190 characters or less.
---@field recipient_file_error? string Why the recipient file failed, when `recipient_file_status` is `error`. Customer.io omits it otherwise.
---@field recipient_file_status? customerio.ScheduleNewsletterResponseNewsletterRecipientFileStatus How far Customer.io has got with a recipient file, whether you uploaded it in the UI or passed `recipient_file_url`. Customer.io omits this field when the audience came from a `recipients` filter. Wait for `done` before you send or schedule.
---@field recipient_segment_ids? customerio.ScheduleNewsletterResponseNewsletterRecipientSegmentIdsList If the recipient conditions included segments, this returns a list of those segment ids.
---@field scheduled_at? number|distilled.Null When the one-time send is scheduled to go out, or `null` if it has no active schedule. Always included. - A paused one-time send keeps its schedule, so this still has a value while `send_state` is `paused`. - After sending starts, this stores the next scheduled send if there is one, like the next timezone batch or the remainder of an A/B test. - When `tz_match_enabled` is `true`, the `scheduled_at` property is the time you asked for in the [schedule request](/integrations/api/app/tag/send-messages/scheduleNewsletter/) until sending starts. - If you send immediately instead of scheduling, there's a short delay before Customer.io starts sending. During that delay, `scheduled_at` isn't `null` and isn't a future time: it's the time you made the request, an earlier time, or `0`. - While Customer.io [holds the send](/messaging/send/broadcasts/one-time-sends/#queued-sends) until its segments finish building, this is the next time Customer.io checks the segments.
---@field send_state? customerio.ScheduleNewsletterResponseNewsletterSendState Where the one-time send is in its lifecycle. Always included. This matches the status shown in Customer.io, with one exception noted under `awaiting_winner`. - `drafted`: The one-time send isn't scheduled and hasn't been sent. - `scheduled`: A send time is set and sending hasn't started. A scheduled send that Customer.io is holding until its segments finish building also reports `scheduled`. - `sending`: Sending has started and is still in progress. More batches are due (timezone-match batches or the remainder of an A/B test), or messages are still being delivered. A send-now send that Customer.io is holding until its segments finish building also reports `sending`. - `sent`: The one-time send is no longer sending. This doesn't mean that every individual message has reached a final state. - `paused`: The one-time send is paused. - `stopped`: The one-time send was stopped. - `failed`: The one-time send failed. - `awaiting_winner`: An A/B test has started and is waiting for you to pick a winner before the rest of the audience gets it. Test messages might still be going out. Customer.io's UI stops showing "awaiting winner" 30 days after the first send, but the API keeps reporting `awaiting_winner` because you can still pick a winner. When more than one state applies, Customer.io reports the first match in this order: `stopped`, `failed`, `paused`, `awaiting_winner`, `sending`, `sent`, `scheduled`, `drafted`.
---@field sent_at? number|distilled.Null The last time the one-time send was sent, or `null` if it hasn't been sent.
---@field subscription_topic_id? number If you enabled a [subscription center](/journeys/channels/subscriptions/center/) on your workspace, this returns the id of the subscription preference you set.
---@field tags? customerio.ScheduleNewsletterResponseNewsletterTagsList An array of tags associated with the one-time send.
---@field timezone? string The [IANA timezone name](/journeys/send/timezones/example-timezones/#region-format) of the schedule (for example, `America/New_York`). Customer.io includes this field only when the active schedule has a timezone. It's omitted otherwise.
---@field type? customerio.ScheduleNewsletterResponseNewsletterType Channel type for a one-time send or one-time send content variant.
---@field tz_match_enabled? boolean `true` when the pending schedule sends at the same local time in each person's timezone. Customer.io includes this field only while a timezone-match schedule is pending. It's omitted otherwise and is never `false`.
---@field updated? number The date time when the referenced ID was last updated.

---A list of message variants in a one-time send, where a variant is a translation or A/B test.
---@alias customerio.ScheduleNewsletterResponseNewsletterContentIdsList number[]

---How far Customer.io has got with a recipient file, whether you uploaded it in the UI or passed `recipient_file_url`. Customer.io omits this field when the audience came from a `recipients` filter. Wait for `done` before you send or schedule.
---@alias customerio.ScheduleNewsletterResponseNewsletterRecipientFileStatus "pending"|"processing"|"done"|"error"

---If the recipient conditions included segments, this returns a list of those segment ids.
---@alias customerio.ScheduleNewsletterResponseNewsletterRecipientSegmentIdsList number[]

---Where the one-time send is in its lifecycle. Always included. This matches the status shown in Customer.io, with one exception noted under `awaiting_winner`.
---
---- `drafted`: The one-time send isn't scheduled and hasn't been sent.
---- `scheduled`: A send time is set and sending hasn't started. A scheduled send that Customer.io is holding until its segments finish building also reports `scheduled`.
---- `sending`: Sending has started and is still in progress. More batches are due (timezone-match batches or the remainder of an A/B test), or messages are still being delivered. A send-now send that Customer.io is holding until its segments finish building also reports `sending`.
---- `sent`: The one-time send is no longer sending. This doesn't mean that every individual message has reached a final state.
---- `paused`: The one-time send is paused.
---- `stopped`: The one-time send was stopped.
---- `failed`: The one-time send failed.
---- `awaiting_winner`: An A/B test has started and is waiting for you to pick a winner before the rest of the audience gets it. Test messages might still be going out. Customer.io's UI stops showing "awaiting winner" 30 days after the first send, but the API keeps reporting `awaiting_winner` because you can still pick a winner.
---
---When more than one state applies, Customer.io reports the first match in this order: `stopped`, `failed`, `paused`, `awaiting_winner`, `sending`, `sent`, `scheduled`, `drafted`.
---@alias customerio.ScheduleNewsletterResponseNewsletterSendState "drafted"|"scheduled"|"sending"|"sent"|"paused"|"stopped"|"failed"|"awaiting_winner"

---An array of tags associated with the one-time send.
---@alias customerio.ScheduleNewsletterResponseNewsletterTagsList string[]

---Channel type for a one-time send or one-time send content variant.
---@alias customerio.ScheduleNewsletterResponseNewsletterType "email"|"webhook"|"twilio"|"push"|"in_app"|"inbox"

---@class customerio.SendEmailRequest
---@field to string The recipients you want to send to, separated by commas. You can include up to 15 total recipients across `to`, `cc`, and `bcc`, with optional display names in quotes.
---@field attachments? customerio.SendEmailRequestAttachments A dictionary of attachments where the filename is the key and the value is the base64-encoded contents. The filename must include the extension (i.e. `name.csv`). The total size of all attachments must be less than 2 MB.
---@field auto_create? boolean If `true` and your `transactional_message_id` doesn't match a record, Customer.io creates an empty record using that value as the *Trigger Name*. The ID must be a string. If the name already belongs to another channel, the request fails with `400`, and numeric IDs ignore this setting. See [details](/journeys/transactional-email/#auto-create-transactional-message-records).
---@field bcc? string Blind copy message recipients. Supports multiple addresses separated by commas. Your request can contain up to 15 total recipients between the `to`, `cc`, and `bcc` keys.
---@field body? string The HTML body of your message. If you provide a `transactional_message_id`, this overrides the template's body. It's also the fallback when you send AMP email (`body_amp`) to a client that doesn't support AMP.
---@field body_amp? string AMP-enabled content for your email. If a recipient's email client doesn't support AMP, they receive your `body` content instead. Make sure you're [set up to send AMP](/journeys/channels/email/layouts/amp-for-email/) first.
---@field body_plain? string The plaintext body of your message. If you provide `transactional_message_id`, this overrides the template's plaintext body.
---@field cc? string Carbon copy message recipients, separated by commas. Unlike BCC recipients, CC recipients are visible to everyone who receives the message. Their opens, clicks, and bounces count toward your message metrics. CC recipients count toward the limit of 15 total recipients across the `to`, `cc`, and `bcc` keys.
---@field disable_css_preprocessing? boolean Set to `true` to disable CSS preprocessing. This setting overrides the CSS preprocessing setting on the `transactional_message_id` as set in the user interface. Transactional emails have CSS preprocessing enabled by default.
---@field disable_message_retention? boolean If true, the message body is not retained in delivery history. Setting this value overrides the value set in the settings of your `transactional_message_id`.
---@field fake_bcc? boolean If true, rather than sending true copies to BCC addresses, Customer.io sends a copy of the message with the subject line containing the recipient address(es).
---@field from? string The address your email is from. It must be a [verified sender](/journeys/channels/email/deliverability/authentication/). Quote any display name, like `\"Person\" <person@example.com>`. This overrides the template's sender; omit it to use the template or your workspace default.
---@field headers? string A JSON string containing header objects with `name` and `value`. Names and values must be strings, with no non-ASCII characters or spaces. You can't overwrite reserved headers.
---@field identifiers? customerio.SendEmailRequestIdentifiers Identifies the person represented by your transactional message by one of, and only one of, `id`, `email`, or `cio_id`.
---@field language? string Overrides language preferences for the person you want to send your transactional message to. Use one of our [supported two- or four-letter language codes](/journeys/channels/localization/getting-started/#supported-languages).
---@field message_data? customerio.SendEmailRequestMessageDataMap An object containing the key-value pairs referenced using liquid in your message.
---@field preheader? string Also known as "preview text", this is the block block of text that users see next to, or underneath, the subject line in their inbox.
---@field queue_draft? boolean If true, your transactional message is held as a draft in Customer.io and not sent directly to your audience. You must go to the Deliveries and Drafts page to send your message.
---@field reply_to? string The address that recipients can reply to, if different from the `from` address.
---@field send_at? number A unix timestamp (seconds since epoch) determining when the message will be sent. The timestamp can be up to 90 days in the future. If this value is in the past, your message is sent immediately.
---@field send_to_unsubscribed? boolean If false, your message is not sent to unsubscribed recipients. Setting this value overrides the value set in the settings of your `transactional_message_id`.
---@field subject? string The subject line for your message. If you provide `transactional_message_id`, this overrides the template's subject.
---@field tracked? boolean If true, Customer.io tracks opens and link clicks in your message.
---@field transactional_message_id? customerio.SendEmailRequestTransactionalMessageId The transactional message template you want to use. You can call the template by its numerical ID or by the *Trigger Name* that you assigned to the template in the UI (case insensitive).

---A dictionary of attachments where the filename is the key and the value is the base64-encoded contents. The filename must include the extension (i.e. `name.csv`). The total size of all attachments must be less than 2 MB.
---@class customerio.SendEmailRequestAttachments
---@field _file_name_? string

---Identifies the person represented by your transactional message by one of, and only one of, `id`, `email`, or `cio_id`.
---@alias customerio.SendEmailRequestIdentifiers customerio.SendEmailRequestIdentifiersCase0|customerio.SendEmailRequestIdentifiersCase1|customerio.SendEmailRequestIdentifiersCase2

---@class customerio.SendEmailRequestIdentifiersCase0
---@field id string The identifier for the person represented by the transactional message. **NOTE**: If your workspace identifies people by email, use the `email` identifier instead.

---@class customerio.SendEmailRequestIdentifiersCase1
---@field email string The identifier for the person represented by the transactional message. Use this option if your workspace identifies people by email rather than by `id`.

---@class customerio.SendEmailRequestIdentifiersCase2
---@field cio_id string A unique, immutable identifier for a person, set by Customer.io when you add a person.

---An object containing the key-value pairs referenced using liquid in your message.
---@alias customerio.SendEmailRequestMessageDataMap { [string]: any }

---The transactional message template you want to use. You can call the template by its numerical ID or by the *Trigger Name* that you assigned to the template in the UI (case insensitive).
---@alias customerio.SendEmailRequestTransactionalMessageId number|string

---@class customerio.SendEmailResponse
---@field delivery_id? string A unique identifier for the message.
---@field queued_at? number A Unix timestamp for when Customer.io accepted and queued your request. For scheduled messages (using `send_at`), this is when we received your request, not when the message sends.
---@field send_at? number For a scheduled message, the Unix timestamp when the message is set to send. Returned only when you provide a future `send_at`.

---@class customerio.SendInAppRequest
---@field identifiers customerio.SendInAppRequestIdentifiers Identifies the person represented by your transactional message by one of, and only one of, `id`, `email`, or `cio_id`.
---@field transactional_message_id customerio.SendInAppRequestTransactionalMessageId The transactional message template that you want to use for your message. You can call the template by its numerical ID or by the *Trigger Name* that you assigned to the template in the UI (case insensitive).
---@field auto_create? boolean Accepted for in-app sends, but of limited use. An auto-created record has no content or layout until you populate it in the UI, so we recommend [creating the in-app message in the UI](/journeys/send/transactional/in-app/) instead. Your `transactional_message_id` must be a new string.
---@field language? string Overrides language preferences for the person you want to send your transactional message to. Use one of our [supported two- or four-letter language codes](/journeys/channels/localization/getting-started/#supported-languages).
---@field message_data? customerio.SendInAppRequestMessageDataMap An object containing the key-value pairs referenced using liquid in the format `{{trigger.<property_name>}}` in your in-app message. These values populate the `properties` field in the message that the SDK delivers to your application.
---@field queue_draft? boolean If true, your transactional message is held as a draft in Customer.io and not sent directly to your audience. You must go to the Deliveries and Drafts page to send your message.
---@field send_at? number A unix timestamp (seconds since epoch) determining when the message will be sent. The timestamp can be up to 90 days in the future. If this value is in the past, your message is sent immediately.

---Identifies the person represented by your transactional message by one of, and only one of, `id`, `email`, or `cio_id`.
---@alias customerio.SendInAppRequestIdentifiers customerio.SendInAppRequestIdentifiersCase0|customerio.SendInAppRequestIdentifiersCase1|customerio.SendInAppRequestIdentifiersCase2

---@class customerio.SendInAppRequestIdentifiersCase0
