---Check a sync's folder now
---
---Check the sync's folder for new files now, instead of waiting for the next scheduled check. If a check is already running, Customer.io doesn't start another. Customer.io responds right away, before the check finishes. Use [look up a collection's sync](/integrations/api/app/tag/collections/getCollectionSync/) to see the result.
---
---`POST /v1/collections/{collection_id}/sync/poll`
---Errors: BadRequest, NotFound.
---@param input customerio.PollCollectionSyncRequest
---@return customerio.PollCollectionSyncResponse? value
---@return distilled.Error? err
function Client:poll_collection_sync(input) end

---Suppress an email at the ESP
---
---Suppress an email address at the email service provider (ESP). Addresses suppressed this way are only suppressed through the ESP; these adresses are _not_ suppressed in Customer.io, so the person can remain in your workspace (though emails to the address would be blocked at the ESP).
---
---`POST /v1/esp/suppression/{suppression_type}/{email_address}`
---@param input customerio.PostSuppressionRequest
---@return any? value
---@return distilled.Error? err
function Client:post_suppression(input) end

---Sync a collection from a source
---
---Watch a folder in one of your [collection sources](/integrations/api/app/tag/collections/createCollectionSource/), and have Customer.io load each new file in the folder into the collection. Customer.io saves and deletes rows by key, the same way as a [write operation](/integrations/api/app/tag/collections/submitCollectionWrite/), so the collection needs a key field. A collection can have exactly one sync.
---
---Each time Customer.io checks the folder, it loads new files in alphabetical order of their paths, up to 2,000 files at a time. Customer.io remembers the last file it loaded and only loads files whose paths sort after it. Name your files so that newer files sort later, like by starting each name with the date (`2026-11-03-products.csv`). Customer.io never loads a file whose path sorts before the last file it loaded, or a file that you overwrite at a path it already loaded. To send changed rows, write a new file.
---
---Customer.io checks each file before it saves any of the file's rows. Customer.io loads the valid rows and records rejected rows, which you can look up with [list rejected sync rows](/integrations/api/app/tag/collections/getCollectionSyncRejects/). If two rows in a file have the same key, Customer.io loads the first one and rejects the second. If Customer.io can't load a whole file, like when the file is too large or isn't JSON or CSV, the sync's `state` becomes `failed`, and Customer.io tries that file again at each later check. To get the sync going again, overwrite the file with one that Customer.io can load, or delete the file.
---
---To change a sync, send it again. If you change only `poll_hours`, the sync keeps its place. If you change the source or the `prefix`, the sync starts over from the first file in the folder.
---
---`PUT /v1/collections/{collection_id}/sync`
---Errors: BadRequest, NotFound, UnprocessableEntity.
---@param input customerio.PutCollectionSyncRequest
---@return customerio.PutCollectionSyncResponse? value
---@return distilled.Error? err
function Client:put_collection_sync(input) end

---Schedule a one-time send
---
---Schedule a one-time send (referred to as a "newsletter" in our APIs) to send at a specific time. It must be in a draft state. If it has already been sent, you'll get a `400` error. If it's already scheduled, this endpoint updates the scheduled time.
---
---The response returns the one-time send, so you can read back the schedule you set: `send_state` and `scheduled_at` are always included, and `timezone` and `tz_match_enabled` are included when they apply.
---
---To retrieve the schedule parameters after scheduling with [Get a one-time send](/integrations/api/app/tag/newsletters/getNewsletters/). After you start a send with timezone-match, `scheduled_at` reports the next timezone batch or the remainder of an A/B test instead of the time you requested.
---
---If a recipient segment is still building at the scheduled time, Customer.io [holds the send](/messaging/send/broadcasts/one-time-sends/#queued-sends) until the segment finishes. While Customer.io holds it, `send_state` stays `scheduled` and `scheduled_at` is the next time Customer.io checks the segments. If the segments don't finish building within 6 hours, the one-time send returns to `drafted` without sending any messages. A schedule with `tz_match_enabled` set to `true` doesn't wait, so you can't make that request while a recipient segment is still building.
---
---The recipients are fixed when you create the one-time send. If you're not sure who will receive it, you can use the [List one-time sends](/integrations/api/app/tag/newsletters/listNewsletters/) endpoint to get a list of one-time sends and their recipients.
---
---`POST /v1/newsletters/{newsletter_id}/schedule`
---Errors: BadRequest, NotFound, UnprocessableEntity.
---@param input customerio.ScheduleNewsletterRequest
---@return customerio.ScheduleNewsletterResponse? value
---@return distilled.Error? err
function Client:schedule_newsletter(input) end

---Send a transactional email
---
---Send a transactional email. While not strictly required, we recommend that you include a `transactional_message_id` in your request. If you don't, Customer.io attributes metrics to `"transactional_message_id": 1`, so multiple messages can roll up under the same ID.
---
---If this is the first time you send a message with the API, you can include the `auto_create` parameter along with a `transactional_message_id` string to create a record for you.
---
---You can also include a `body`, `subject`, and `from` values to override the message template. Or, if you create your message entirely through the API, you *must* include these values because your `transactional_message_id` won't have any content. See [Examples and API parameters](/journeys/send/transactional/email/#auto-create-transactional-message-records) for more details.
---
---`POST /v1/send/email`
---Errors: BadRequest, Forbidden.
---@param input customerio.SendEmailRequest
---@return customerio.SendEmailResponse? value
---@return distilled.Error? err
function Client:send_email(input) end

---Send a transactional in-app message
---
---Send a transactional in-app message. In-app messages render in your application through the Customer.io SDK to the devices associated with the person you target by `identifiers`.
---
---You send a message using a `transactional_message_id` for an in-app message template that you've set up in the user interface. The `transactional_message_id` can be either the numerical ID for the template or the *Trigger Name* that you assigned the template.
---
---You can find your `transactional_message_id` from the code sample in the **Overview** tab for your transactional message in the user interface, or you can look up a list of your transactional messages through the [App API](/integrations/api/app/tag/transactional/).
---
---**Note**: Your workspace must have in-app messaging enabled. Requests sent to a workspace without the in-app capability return `403`.
---
---`POST /v1/send/in_app`
---Errors: BadRequest, Forbidden.
---@param input customerio.SendInAppRequest
---@return customerio.SendInAppResponse? value
---@return distilled.Error? err
function Client:send_in_app(input) end

---Send a transactional inbox message
---
---Send a transactional inbox message. Inbox messages deliver raw JSON payloads to your application through our JavaScript SDK, allowing you to build custom notification centers, message feeds, and other UI components.
---
---You send a message using a `transactional_message_id` for an inbox message template created in the user interface. The `transactional_message_id` can be either the numerical ID for the template or the *Trigger Name* that you assigned the template.
---
---You can find your `transactional_message_id` from the code sample in the **Overview** tab for your transactional message in the user interface, or you can look up a list of your transactional messages through the [App API](/integrations/api/app/tag/transactional/).
---
---**Note**: Inbox messages are currently available for web platforms only and require the Customer.io In-App Plugin to be installed.
---
---`POST /v1/send/inbox_message`
---Errors: BadRequest, Forbidden.
---@param input customerio.SendInboxMessageRequest
---@return customerio.SendInboxMessageResponse? value
---@return distilled.Error? err
function Client:send_inbox_message(input) end

---Trigger a one-time send
---
---Trigger a one-time send (referred to as a "newsletter" in our APIs) immediately. It must be in a draft state. If it has already been sent, you'll get a `400` error. The recipients are fixed when you create the one-time send. If you're not sure who will receive it, you can use the [List one-time sends](/integrations/api/app/tag/newsletters/listNewsletters/) endpoint to get a list of one-time sends and their recipients.
---
---If the recipients include a segment that's still building, Customer.io accepts the request and [holds the send](/messaging/send/broadcasts/one-time-sends/#queued-sends) until the segment finishes. While Customer.io holds it, `send_state` is `sending` and `scheduled_at` is the next time Customer.io checks the segments. If the segments don't finish building within 6 hours, the one-time send fails without sending any messages.
---
---To reschedule a one-time send, use the [Schedule a one-time send](/integrations/api/app/tag/send-messages/scheduleNewsletter/) endpoint instead.
---
---`POST /v1/newsletters/{newsletter_id}/send`
---Errors: BadRequest, NotFound, UnprocessableEntity.
---@param input customerio.SendNewsletterRequest
---@return customerio.SendNewsletterResponse? value
---@return distilled.Error? err
function Client:send_newsletter(input) end

---Send a transactional push
---
---Send a transactional push. You send a message using a `transactional_message_id` for a transactional push message template composed in the user interface. You can optionally override any of the template values at send time. The `transactional_message_id` can be either the numerical ID for the template or the *Trigger Name* that you assigned the template.
---
---You can find your `transactional_message_id` from the code sample in the **Overview** tab for your transactional message in the user interface, or you can look up a list of your transactional messages through the [App API](/integrations/api/app/tag/transactional/).
---
---`POST /v1/send/push`
---Errors: BadRequest, Forbidden.
---@param input customerio.SendPushRequest
---@return customerio.SendPushResponse? value
---@return distilled.Error? err
function Client:send_push(input) end

---Send a transactional SMS
---
---Send a transactional SMS message. To send a message, you'll need to provide a `transactional_message_id`. This is either the numerical ID of your transactional message template or the *Trigger Name* that you assigned the template.
---
---You can find your `transactional_message_id` from the code sample in the **Overview** tab for your transactional message in the user interface, or you can look up a list of your transactional messages through the [App API](/integrations/api/app/tag/transactional/).
---
---`POST /v1/send/sms`
---Errors: BadRequest, Forbidden.
---@param input customerio.SendSMSRequest
---@return customerio.SendSMSResponse? value
---@return distilled.Error? err
function Client:send_sms(input) end

---Send a transactional WhatsApp message
---
---Send a transactional WhatsApp message. To use this endpoint, you must be integrated with [WhatsApp Business](/messaging/send/transactional/whatsapp/#before-you-begin), not Twilio. Then you need to [create the template in the UI and have it approved by WhatsApp/Meta](/messaging/design-studio/whatsapp-design-studio/) before sending it.
---
---To send a message, you need to provide a `transactional_message_id`. This is either the numerical ID of your transactional message template or the *Trigger Name* that you assigned the template.
---
---You can find your `transactional_message_id` from the code sample in the **Overview** tab for your transactional message in the user interface, or you can look up a list of your transactional messages through the [App API](/integrations/api/app/tag/transactional/listTransactional/).
---
---`POST /v1/send/whatsapp`
---Errors: BadRequest, Forbidden.
---@param input customerio.SendWhatsAppRequest
---@return customerio.SendWhatsAppResponse? value
---@return distilled.Error? err
function Client:send_whats_app(input) end

---Start a live notification
---
---Start a live notification for a profile. Customer.io mints an `instance_id` for the new activity and returns it. You'll use this ID to update, end, or check the status of the activity.
---
---[On iOS](/integrations/sdk/ios/live-activities/set-up/#step-5-start-an-activity), this delivers a push-to-start event through APNs, so the person's device must have a registered push-to-start token for the `notification_type` (the SDK registers one automatically when your app registers the activity type). Push-to-start requires iOS 17.2 or later. iOS starts also require a `push_payload.alert` with a title and body.
---
---[On Android](/integrations/sdk/android/live-notifications/set-up/#step-4-start-a-notification), this delivers an FCM data message, which the SDK renders without your app being open.
---
---`POST /v1/live_notifications/start`
---Errors: BadRequest, NotFound.
---@param input customerio.StartLiveNotificationRequest
---@return customerio.StartLiveNotificationResponse? value
---@return distilled.Error? err
function Client:start_live_notification(input) end

---Write to a collection
---
---Add, update, or delete rows by key, without replacing the whole collection. The collection needs a [key field](/messaging/objects-data/collections/#key-fields), which you can set when you [update a collection](/integrations/api/app/tag/collections/updateCollection/). A row whose key already exists replaces the old row, and a row with a new key is added. To replace every row instead, use [update a collection](/integrations/api/app/tag/collections/updateCollection/).
---
---To delete rows, give the collection a `delete_field`, like `deleted`. A row that sets that field to `true` deletes the row with the same key. A row that doesn't set it to `true` is saved with the field as you sent it. One write request can save some rows and delete others.
---
---Send the rows in one of three ways: in the request as `data`, as a file at a `url`, or as a file in one of your [collection sources](/integrations/api/app/tag/collections/listCollectionSources/) with `source_id` and `path`. Customer.io reads a file when the write runs, so a file can hold far more rows than a request body can.
---
---Customer.io queues the write and responds right away with a write `id`, so large writes don't time out. Customer.io applies writes to a collection one at a time, in the order it receives them. Use [look up a collection write](/integrations/api/app/tag/collections/getCollectionWrite/) to check the result.
---
---Customer.io checks every row before it saves anything. If any row is invalid, Customer.io doesn't save any of them. Customer.io can handle a write of up to 500 rows all at once. If your request is larger, we apply writes 500 rows at a time. While a large write runs, a read can return some of its rows and not others. If a large write fails partway, the rows it saved stay in the collection, and you can safely send the same write again.
---
---`POST /v1/collections/{collection_id}/writes`
---Errors: NotFound, UnprocessableEntity.
---@param input customerio.SubmitCollectionWriteRequest
---@return customerio.SubmitCollectionWriteResponse? value
---@return distilled.Error? err
function Client:submit_collection_write(input) end

---Get transactional message link metrics
---
---Returns metrics for clicked links from a transactional message, both in total and in `series` periods (days, weeks, etc). `series` metrics are ordered oldest to newest (i.e. the 0-index for any result is the oldest step/period).
---
---You cannot request fewer than 2 steps of any period (2 hours, 2 days, 2 weeks, or 2 months). For instance, `?period=days&steps=1` means two days - the 48 hours before the API request was made. `?period=days&steps=0` returns the same as the maximum of the period - `?period=days&steps=45`. See the `steps` parameter below for the maximum count of each period.
---
---`GET /v1/transactional/{transactional_id}/metrics/links`
---Errors: NotFound.
---@param input customerio.TransactionalLinksRequest
---@return customerio.TransactionalLinksResponse? value
---@return distilled.Error? err
function Client:transactional_links(input) end

---Get transactional message deliveries
---
---Returns information about the deliveries (instances of messages sent to individual people) from a transactional message. Provide query parameters to refine the metrics you want to return.
---
---Use the `start_ts` and `end_ts` to find messages within a time range. If your request doesn't include `start_ts` and `end_ts` parameters, we'll return the most recent 6 months of messages. If your `start_ts` and `end_ts` range is more than 12 months, we'll return 12 months of data from the most recent timestamp in your request. Timestamps reflect when deliveries were created in our system, not when they were actually sent to recipients. There may be a delay between creation and sending.
---
---`GET /v1/transactional/{transactional_id}/messages`
---Errors: NotFound.
---@param input customerio.TransactionalMessagesRequest
---@return customerio.TransactionalMessagesResponse? value
---@return distilled.Error? err
function Client:transactional_messages(input) end

---Get transactional message metrics
---
---Returns a list of metrics for a transactional message in `steps` (days, weeks, etc). We return metrics from oldest to newest (i.e. the 0-index for any result is the oldest step/period).
---
---You cannot request fewer than 2 steps of any period (2 hours, 2 days, 2 weeks, or 2 months). For instance, `?period=days&steps=1` means two days - the 48 hours before the API request was made. `?period=days&steps=0` returns the same as the maximum of the period - `?period=days&steps=45`. See the `steps` parameter below for the maximum count of each period.
---
---`GET /v1/transactional/{transactional_id}/metrics`
---Errors: NotFound.
---@param input customerio.TransactionalMetricsRequest
---@return customerio.TransactionalMetricsResponse? value
---@return distilled.Error? err
function Client:transactional_metrics(input) end

---Send an API-triggered broadcast
---
---Trigger a broadcast and optionally provide data to populate liquid placeholders in the message. To trigger one-time send broadcasts, use the [one-time send endpoint](/integrations/api/app/tag/send-messages/sendNewsletter/).
---
---Broadcasts are optimized to send messages to a large audience and not for one-to-one interactions. Use our [transactional API](/integrations/api/app/tag/send-messages/sendEmail/) or [event-triggered automations](/journeys/send/campaigns/triggers/#event-trigger) to respond to your audience on an individual, one-to-one basis.
---
---**Define your audience**
---
---**The shape of the request depends on how you define your audience**: default (the recipients set in the UI), custom conditions, a list of emails, a list of customer IDs, a map of users, or a data file.
---
---You can narrow any of these audiences, except the default audience, with the `filter` object. This is useful when a file or a list of people is broader than the audience you want for your broadcast.
---
---**You can only trigger broadcasts to send to people you've already added to your workspace.** A broadcast cannot add or identify new people. If you reference people who don't exist in your broadcast audience, the broadcast will fail by default. You can override this behavior by setting the `email_ignore_missing` and/or `id_ignore_missing` flags to `true`. The broadcast will skip over any people who don't exist in your workspace and send to the remaining recipients.
---
---You can reference properties in the `data` object in your broadcast using liquid—`{{trigger.<property_in_data_obj>}}`.
---
---**Send or schedule your broadcast**
---
---By default, we send your broadcast as soon as you trigger it. Set `scheduled_at` to send it at a future time instead. You can [cancel the trigger](/integrations/api/app/tag/send-messages/cancelBroadcastTrigger/) to stop a scheduled send before it starts sending. You can't change a trigger's `scheduled_at` value later; if you need to reschedule your broadcast, you must cancel the trigger and re-trigger the broadcast with the new timestamp.
---
---If your broadcast produces a `422` error, you can [get more information about the errors](/integrations/api/app/tag/broadcasts/broadcastErrors/) to see what went wrong.
---
---**This endpoint is rate-limited to one request every 10 seconds.** After exceeding this, you'll receive a status of `429`. Learn more about [API-triggered broadcast limits](/integrations/api/app/tag/send-messages/) above. If you need to trigger broadcasts more often, contact us to talk through your use case.
---
---`POST /v1/campaigns/{broadcast_id}/triggers`
---Errors: NotFound, UnprocessableEntity.
---@param input customerio.TriggerBroadcastRequest
---@return customerio.TriggerBroadcastResponse? value
---@return distilled.Error? err
function Client:trigger_broadcast(input) end

